# Clean Claude Orphans

Reconciles installed Claude Code state (MCP servers, plugins, plugin marketplaces, skills)
against `packages.yaml`, starting from `audit-packages`'s findings. For every orphan, decide
**REMOVE**, **KEEP**, or **DECLARE** — confirm the full plan with the user before removing
anything.

Use the `chezmoi` skill to DECLARE an item back into `packages.yaml`. For the mirror-image
case — a declared item locally disabled via `skillOverrides`/`enabledPlugins` — run
`check-claude-overrides` instead.
{{- if $claudeEnvs }}

## This machine runs multiple Claude Code environments

- **Skills are shared** across every environment (symlinked) — removing one removes it
  everywhere.
- **MCP servers and plugins are NOT shared** — each environment has independent state, and a
  `claude` command only sees the environment its own `$CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR` names. Check and
  clean every environment Step 1 lists under `## environments`, prefixing commands with
  `CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR=<path>` for any that isn't current. Don't call the machine clean after
  checking only the current one.
{{- end }}

## Step 1: Gather orphans and context

```bash
~/.claude/skills/clean-claude-orphans/scripts/list-claude-orphans.sh
```

Read-only. Output is JSON Lines under five `##` sections:

- `## environments` — `{path, current}` per declared Claude Code environment on this machine.
- `## {{ $localMarketplaceName }}-plugins` — this repo's own marketplace catalog, `{name, skills}`;
  used to spot skill/MCP-server orphans that duplicate a bundle instead.
- `## chezmoi-managed-skills` — `{name}` for skills deployed by `chezmoi apply` rather than
  `npx skills add`; these never appear in `## orphans`.
- `## installed-plugins` — `{id, scope, projectPath, chezmoi_personal, enabled}` for every
  installed plugin. `chezmoi_personal` (id ends `@{{ $localMarketplaceName }}`) is never a DECLARE
  candidate. Needed before uninstalling anything — see Step 3. The library re-derives a
  project/local-scope entry's `enabled` by re-running the check with `cwd` inside that plugin's
  own `projectPath` (`claude plugins list --json` otherwise misreports this when run from
  elsewhere) — so this field should already be trustworthy, but if a verdict here still looks
  surprising, confirm by hand the same way before removing anything: `cd <projectPath> && claude
  plugins list --json | jq '.[] | select(.id=="<id>")'`.
- `## orphans` — `{category, item, skill_tag}`, category one of `mcp_server` / `plugin` /
  `marketplace` / `skill`. Already excludes chezmoi-managed skills and
  `@{{ $localMarketplaceName }}` plugins. `skill_tag: bundled-by:<plugin>` marks a standalone skill
  that duplicates one bundled by a {{ $localMarketplaceName }} plugin.

If the user already pasted `audit-packages` output, use it for `## orphans` but still run the
script for the other sections.

## Step 2: Classify each orphan

Work marketplaces last — removing one can orphan the plugins installed from it.

### MCP servers
| Check | Verdict |
|---|---|
| Still declared in a project's own `.mcp.json` you use | **DECLARE** in packages.yaml |
| Duplicates a server a {{ $localMarketplaceName }} plugin already registers (see `## {{ $localMarketplaceName }}-plugins`) | **REMOVE** the manual entry — re-adding is a one-line `enabledPlugins` toggle |
| Genuinely unused | **REMOVE**: `claude mcp remove <name>` |

### Plugins
| Check | Verdict |
|---|---|
| `enabled: true` in `## installed-plugins` | **KEEP** (or **DECLARE** if it should be standard) |
| A {{ $localMarketplaceName }} plugin already covers the same functionality under a different name | **REMOVE**, recommend switching — re-adding is a one-line enable |
| Genuinely unused | **REMOVE**: `claude plugins uninstall <id>` (scope procedure in Step 3) |

### Plugin marketplaces
| Check | Verdict |
|---|---|
| Still has a KEPT plugin installed from it (`.id` ends `@<marketplace>`) | **KEEP** until those plugins are removed first |
| No remaining plugins | **REMOVE**: `claude plugins marketplace remove <name>` — use the registered `name` from `claude plugins marketplace list --json`, not the raw `item` string, if `item` is a git/GitHub URL |

`{{ $localMarketplaceName }}` itself is declared in `packages.yaml`, so it never appears in `## orphans`.

### Skills
| Check | Verdict |
|---|---|
| Referenced by name inside another skill or plugin you're keeping | **KEEP** — it's a dependency |
| `skill_tag` is `bundled-by:<plugin>` | **REMOVE** the standalone copy, keep the plugin-provided one |
| `audit-packages`'s match is a heuristic, not ground truth | Skim the skill's own `SKILL.md`/README before trusting a "covered by collection" note |
| Genuinely unused | **REMOVE**: `rm -rf ~/.claude/skills/<name>`{{ if $claudeEnvs }} (affects every environment){{ end }} |

## Step 2.5: Unclutter — duplicates beyond the orphan list

The checks above also apply outside `## orphans`: something can duplicate a
{{ $localMarketplaceName }} plugin while still being correctly declared (so `audit-packages` never
flags it). Skim what's actually installed — `claude plugins list --json`, `ls
~/.claude/skills/`, `claude mcp list` — against `## {{ $localMarketplaceName }}-plugins`.

Also check `## installed-plugins` for the same plugin `id` installed at **both** `user` scope
and `project`/`local` scope (e.g. a `project`-scope entry created by running `claude` from
`$HOME` itself) — the project/local entry adds nothing once the plugin is already installed at
user scope, so it's a decluttering candidate: offer to clear its `enabledPlugins` entry (Step
3's scope procedure) even though the plugin itself isn't an orphan.

Fold any match into the same confirm-then-execute plan as Step 3 — don't run this separately.

## Step 3: Present the plan, confirm, then execute

List every orphan (and Step 2.5 candidate) with its verdict and a one-line reason. Confirm the
whole plan, or adjust specific items, before running anything destructive. Remove plugins
sourced from a marketplace before removing that marketplace. For DECLARE items, edit
`packages.yaml` via the `chezmoi` skill instead of touching installed state.

### Removing a plugin: scope determines the procedure

`## installed-plugins` already gives each plugin's `scope` and (for project/local) `projectPath`:

- **`user` scope**: `claude plugins uninstall <id>` works directly, from anywhere.
- **`project`/`local` scope**: first remove its `enabledPlugins` entry from the owning
  project's `.claude/settings.json` (project scope) or `.claude/settings.local.json` (local
  scope) — the file lives at that `projectPath`. Then run `claude plugins uninstall <id>
  --scope <project|local>` with your working directory inside `projectPath` (scope resolution
  is cwd-relative).

## Step 4: Verify

Re-run `audit-packages` (or the Step 1 script). Anything still listed should have an
explanation — most often a plugin still enabled by a project that wasn't checked.

## Common mistakes

- Uninstalling a project/local-scope plugin with a bare `claude plugins uninstall <id>` and no
  `--scope` flag, or from outside its owning `projectPath` — both fail. See Step 3.
- Removing the `{{ $localMarketplaceName }}` marketplace.
{{- if $claudeEnvs }}
- Treating a skill orphan as machine-local — removing it removes it from every environment.
- Checking only the current `$CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR` and calling the machine clean while sibling
  environments still carry orphans.
{{- end }}
- Only scanning `## orphans` and missing Step 2.5 duplicates that are correctly declared but
  still redundant with a {{ $localMarketplaceName }} plugin.
- Passing a marketplace orphan's raw `item` string straight to `claude plugins marketplace
  remove` when it's a git/GitHub URL — use the registered `name` from `claude plugins
  marketplace list --json` instead.
{{ end -}}
